abor Day polling or a late consolidation of Republicans and independents behind Biggs, aided by controversies like the Charlie Kirk veto, could tighten the race quickly.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Katie Hobbs (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's governor race pits incumbent Democrat Katie Hobbs against former Republican congressman Andy Biggs, with Green and Natural Law nominees also on the ballot. Polling through mid-August showed Hobbs ahead by high single digits on average, with several surveys putting her lead in double digits as independents and women broke her way. Prediction markets price her chances near 88 percent, and most race raters call the contest Lean Democratic, with one going Likely Democratic.\n\nHobbs also holds a clear money advantage, having raised about $10 million to Biggs's $3.2 million. The state leans slightly Republican, and Biggs is working to define himself for a statewide audience, while Hobbs has courted moderate voters and drawn criticism for vetoes including bills honoring Charlie Kirk. Polls are several weeks old, leaving room for the race to tighten, but the accumulated evidence favors Hobbs.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9144, "margin": 7.07}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9047, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9201, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9198, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8839, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8882, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8963, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8934, "margin": 6.5}]}